  <header class="qa-header">
    <p class="qa-pillar">Species Sub-page · SPC·10d</p>
    <h1>Fan-tailed Raven Diet: What This Species Eats</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Fan-tailed Raven (<em>Corvus rhipidurus</em>) is a broad omnivore. Invertebrates, grain and seeds, fruit, carrion, small vertebrates, and human food waste make up its diet. The species favours cliff and rocky habitats and is highly synanthropic — closely associated with human settlements across the Horn of Africa, East Africa, the Middle East, and the Arabian Peninsula.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Primary Food Items</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Based on HBW species account:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Invertebrates:</strong> beetles, grasshoppers, caterpillars, termites — ground-foraging and foraging on cliff faces and rocky outcrops.</li>
      <li><strong>Grain and seeds:</strong> cereal grains in agricultural areas; native seeds and plant material.</li>
      <li><strong>Fruit:</strong> various fruits and berries taken opportunistically.</li>
      <li><strong>Carrion:</strong> roadkill, dead livestock — a component but less dominant than in larger raven species. The Fan-tailed Raven's bill is proportionally smaller than the Thick-billed or White-necked Raven, limiting its ability to open large carcasses.</li>
      <li><strong>Small vertebrates:</strong> lizards, mice, nestlings and eggs — taken opportunistically.</li>
      <li><strong>Human food waste:</strong> markets, rubbish, village waste — a major and reliable food source throughout the species' range. The Fan-tailed Raven is one of the most visible large birds in highland towns of Ethiopia and Yemen.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Cliff and Rock Foraging</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Fan-tailed Raven is characteristically a cliff-associated species. It nests on cliff faces and rocky outcrops and forages in the talus and rocky ground below, exploiting insects and small animals associated with these habitats. It soars readily from cliff edges to scan surrounding terrain and open country for food. This aerial mobility extends its foraging range considerably beyond the immediate cliff habitat.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Related Pages</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/species/fan-tailed-raven/">Fan-tailed Raven — species hub</a></li>
